### Modifications

The sprite is not byte-identical to the upstream file. Changes made here, as
required by the Apache License 2.0, section 4(b):

- **`information` trimmed.** The upstream symbol repeats its dial and its bar as
  two extra `fill="currentColor"` paths on top of a path that already draws both.
  Those two paths are removed so the glyph is a single shape; the drawn result is
  unchanged.
- **Brand mark replaced.** The retired `kubesphere` glyph is removed from the
  sprite and from `ICON_NAMES`, so nothing can resolve it any more. `kubeaihub`
  now draws the Kube AI Hub mark: a solid hexagon with three connected nodes
  carved out of it, distinct from KubeSphere's segmented one. The cut-outs are
  sub-paths of a single `fill-rule="evenodd"` path rather than a `mask`, so the
  glyph carries no id reference and survives being inlined more than once on a
  page.
- **Duplicate paint paths removed.** Where a symbol ends with a path that repaints,
  in the same ink, a shape an earlier `fill-rule="evenodd"` path already carved
  out, the repaint is dropped and the cut-out shows again. Without this the mark
  and its plate merged into one solid mass — `logout-right-duotone`,
  `login-left-duotone`, `tags-duotone` and `switch-button-duotone` all rendered
  as blobs. `switch-button-duotone` also gains an explicit `.22` track plate so
  its knobs still separate from the rails.
- **White detail fills made transparent.** Twelve symbols painted inner detail in
  a hard-coded `#FFF`/`white` (`golang`, `client`, `cron-job`, `deamon-set`,
  `gateway`, `job`, `springcloud`, `ssd`, `vsan`, `edge`, `crane`,
  `up-circle-duotone`). That detail is invisible on a light surface and wrong on
  any tinted one. Those fills are now `none`, so the surface behind the glyph
  shows through, which is what the artwork intended. `mask` and `clipPath`
  geometry keeps its white: it is not ink.
- **Fixed-palette fills replaced with `currentColor`.** Upstream hard-codes a
  light grey (`#CCD3DB`, `#B6C2CD`), a dark navy (`#36435C`, `#242E42`,
  `#C1C9D1`, `#14215A`) and an off-white (`#F9FBFD`) as secondary or cut-out
  fills. Those values only read correctly on one surface tone, so on a dark theme
  the light greys sank into the background and on a light theme the cut-outs
  disappeared. They are now `currentColor` with `fill-opacity`, which follows the
  surrounding text colour and stays legible on every theme. The drawn shapes are
  unchanged; only how they take their colour changed.
- **Sibling paths folded into one compound.** Around two dozen symbols drew their
  detail as a second path in the same ink as the plate beneath it, so the detail
  repainted what it was meant to carve and the glyph came out a solid slab —
  `login-accounts`, `select`, `mail`, `textarea`, `textfield`, `report`, `port`,
  `minus-square`, `plus-square`, `catalog`, `cpu`, `documentation`, `folder`,
  `user-profile`, `wallet`, `eip-group`, `project`, `insert-chart`, `terminal`,
  `bigdata`, `data`, `deal`, `discount` and `file`. Both `d` strings are now one
  path with `fill-rule="evenodd"`, so the overlap cancels and the detail reads as
  a cut-out. The drawn silhouettes are unchanged.
- **Detail folded into the plate on a further nine symbols.** The same slab
  problem showed up on `eye` (pupil painted over by a same-ink disc), `home`
  (roof line and door), `lock` (shackle and keyhole), `layer` (arrow inside the
  stacked plate), `monitor` (waveform on the bezel), `cron-job` (clock hands drawn
  twice, once filled and once as `fill="none"`) and `job` / `client` (inner badge
  detail left as `fill="none"`, so it never drew at all). Each is now a single
  `evenodd` path whose later sub-paths cut out of the earlier ones.
- **`filter` redrawn.** The upstream funnel and lid were separate filled shapes
  that cancelled where they met, leaving an open skeleton. It is now one
  `evenodd` funnel with a cut-out mouth and a solid stem, which stays readable at
  the 14px size the console shell uses for nav entries.
- **Content rescaled into the 24 grid.** A number of symbols were authored against
  a larger artboard (`user-overview` 20, `crane` 40, the `*-duotone` generation 48)
  or simply occupied a thin band of it (`nodejs`, `sonarqube` were 24-unit wide but
  only ~5 units tall). `<Icon>` always draws a `0 0 24 24` viewBox, so those glyphs
  were either clipped at the edge or shrank to an unreadable sliver. Each is now
  wrapped in a `translate(...) scale(...)` that fits its real ink box inside the
  grid with a margin. The drawn shapes are unchanged; only their placement and
  size are. The same pass covered `terminal`, whose path stacked three copies of
  the window and cancelled against itself; it is redrawn as one `evenodd` window.
- **`sonarqube` and `nodejs` take their official marks.** The upstream glyphs were
  wide, flat strips that could not be enlarged without distortion, so both use the
  vendor mark instead (SonarQube's three rising strokes, Node.js' hexagon), each
  bespoke single-ink paths rather than the upstream drawings.
- **`mnic` redrawn.** Its compound path already carved the port bays and the LED
  row, but seven same-ink paths repainted them on top, so the glyph shipped as a
  solid slab. The repaints are gone and the card is redrawn as one `evenodd` shape
  with a bracket rail, four port bays and two LEDs cut out of it.
- **`allowlist` un-slabbebed, `code` given its frame.** `allowlist` had the same
  repaint problem as `mnic`: its compound path carved the avatar, both text rows
  and both checkmarks, then four same-ink paths filled them back in. The repaints
  are dropped. `code`'s compound drew its frame as a filled rectangle rather than
  a ring, so it could never read as a border; it is redrawn as a ring with the
  chevron and the underline cut out of it.
- **Upstream tag damage repaired.** `springcloud` left two `<g>` open and
  `springcloudgateway` two, which is why neither parsed; `download-box-2-duotone`
  and eight following symbols were never closed at all, so the parser swallowed
  every later icon into them. The dangling `<g>`/`<symbol>` openers are removed,
  the missing closers added, and `springcloudgateway`'s clip rect takes
  `currentColor` instead of a hard-coded `white`. All 369 symbols now parse as
  well-formed XML. `gateway` keeps its four `fill="#fff"` mask fills: white is the
  "show" value inside an SVG mask, so that is geometry, not styling.
- **`restart`, `shutdown`, `bigdata` and `backup` redrawn.** Each was a solid
  silhouette: `restart` and `shutdown` painted a same-ink disc over the ring they
  meant to carve, `bigdata` repeated its bar geometry twice inside one path so the
  bars cancelled against themselves under `evenodd`, and `backup` stacked two
  filled cards with no gap between them. All four are now single `evenodd` paths
  whose interior detail — the rotation arrow, the power glyph, three bars of
  differing height, the transfer arrow — cuts out of the plate.
- **Low-alpha plates get an outline.** Upstream "duotone" symbols imply their
  second plane with `fill-opacity=".22"`, which `currentColor` cannot express:
  on the light theme the plate washes out until it reads as a hole. Every such
  plate now keeps a faint `.14` wash and gains a `currentColor` outline at
  `stroke-opacity=".55"`, so the two planes stay distinguishable in both themes
  without a second colour channel.
- **`logout` redrawn.** The upstream glyph was a closed-book mark whose two halves
  could only be told apart by the legacy `color`/`fill` two-channel styling this
  package does not reproduce, so it rendered as a plain rectangle. A single-ink
  `logout` cannot express that split, so the glyph is replaced with the
  conventional meaning — a doorway open to the right with an arrow leaving — drawn
  as one `evenodd` path. `login-accounts` keeps its box-and-person mark.
- **Second un-slabbing pass.** A further twenty symbols had the same repaint
  defect found by rendering every symbol's parts separately: a plate path carved
  its interior detail, then a sibling path in the same ink filled the cut-outs
  back in. `success`, `question`, `radio` and `safe-notice` painted their tick,
  question mark and inner disc on top of the ring that had already carved them;
  `textfield`, `report`, `port` and `trash` repeated a whole sub-shape twice
  inside one path so the two copies cancelled; `user-overview` and `vsan`
  repainted their busts and disc; `loading-circle-duotone` stacked four arcs that
  swallowed the ring. `kafka`, `mongo-db`, `redis`, `database`, `zone`, `vnas`,
  `wallet`, `shield`, `cpu` and `text` were redrawn as single `evenodd` compounds
  whose detail cuts out of the plate. The drawn silhouettes are unchanged.
- **Everything else is unmodified.** Apart from the brand mark and the colour
  substitution above, the remaining symbols are byte-identical to upstream, in the
  same order. `timed-task`, `clock`, `calendar` and `history-duotone` are among them.

Upstream duplicate ids (`barchart`, `springcloud`, `stretch`, `event` each appear
twice) are reproduced as-is. They are an upstream quirk rather than a change made
here: `ICON_NAMES` lists each id once because a browser resolves `<use>` to the
last definition, so the duplicate is unreachable.

Three symbols are not well-formed in upstream and are left that way rather than
guessed at, because no rendering of them is known to be correct: `springcloud`
and `springcloudgateway` contain unbalanced `<g>` elements, and
`download-box-2-duotone` closes with a bare `<symbol>` instead of `</symbol>`.
A bare `<symbol>` opener appears ten times upstream and swallows whatever follows
it, so these glyphs may fail to render; fixing them needs the original artwork,
not a repair of the markup alone.

